#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-context — how full a Claude session's context REALLY is, read off its own transcript.

  cc-context                       the session running in $PWD (newest transcript for this cwd)
  cc-context <repo> <track>        that track's session (session id from the board)
  cc-context --transcript PATH | --session ID
  cc-context --true <target>       ask the session ITSELF: types /context into that idle pane and reads its
                                   number. A busy pane falls back to THAT TARGET'S estimate below (a bare
                                   `repo/track` is the same pair as the two-word form); a target that names no
                                   <repo>/<track> is refused rather than answered about the caller's own cwd.
                                   Everything downstream — the line, --json, --over, the level — then reads THAT
                                   number, so the handoff thresholds are decided on the pane's own reading
  cc-context --all                 TSV of every recorded session: repo track pct used window src age level pending
  cc-context --record              stdin = a Stop hook payload -> measure, record, and tell the session
  cc-context --thresholds          "<journal> <handoff> <ceiling>" (percentages)
  cc-context --handoffs [--json]   the durable handoff record: one countable line per crossing
  cc-context selfcheck             fixtures for the parser, the window chain and the record store
    also: --json · -q (exit code only) · --over [PCT] (exit 0 at/over, 1 under, 2 unmeasurable;
          bare = that session's own handoff line, floors and window included)

THREE LINES, EACH A PERCENTAGE OF THE WINDOW WITH A FLOOR UNDER IT (all six in ~/.cc/config):
  30%, never under 60k   journal — the session is told, once, to append its handoff entry now
  40%, never under 90k   hand off — due, but only when nothing is in flight
  60%                    ceiling — hand off regardless of what is in flight
                         CC_CONTEXT_{WARN,HANDOFF}_PCT · CC_CONTEXT_{WARN,HANDOFF}_MIN · CC_CONTEXT_CEILING_PCT
…AND A RETRY LINE EVERY FIVE PERCENT BETWEEN THE HANDOFF LINE AND THE CEILING (45, 50, 55 on the defaults;
CC_CONTEXT_RETRY_PCT, 0 for none). A handoff can DIE — the one started at 01:35Z on 2026-09-05 sat on a
permission prompt and was abandoned — and until then the session that had taken it had nothing left before the
ceiling: `handed_off` made every later turn 'already taken'. Each of these lines is one more crossing, sent
with --retry, and the ceiling is the last of them so a dead overlap cannot take its teeth with it. Whether a
crossing MEANS a new attempt is cc-handoff's call (its `retryable`: only an overlap that ended with the
predecessor still in charge is tried again); a crossing while one is open is only the watchdog, as before.
On the 1M window the box's sessions run that is 300k / 400k / 600k. They are early on purpose: this is about
how SHARP a session is, not about running out of room. The evidence says there is no plateau to sit just
below — Chroma's "Context Rot" finds degradation at every input length it tested, and NoLiMa has 10 of 12
long-context models below half their short-context score by 32k, a quarter of the advertised window. So the
question is not where the cliff is but how low we can sit and still get work done.

A PERCENTAGE, AND A FLOOR. Degradation tracks ABSOLUTE tokens far more than the fraction used, so a smaller
window does not buy a session more good tokens. But a bare percentage of a small window lands under what a
session needs to do anything (30% of 128k is 38k, most of it prompt and tools). The floor reads: as low as we
can sit, but never so low that a session is handed off before it can work — 60k/90k on a 200k model.

PENDING, NOT IMMEDIATE. Crossing the handoff line sets a handoff PENDING; it does not restart anything. The
restart happens at the first turn where nothing is in flight — no background or scheduled task, no unfinished
merge/rebase, nothing uncommitted or unpushed in any worktree of this repo (see `in_flight`, which is
deliberately pessimistic: a wrong "idle" reading is a handoff in the middle of something, and mid-task state
lives in no file). The 60% ceiling goes anyway: past it, what a degraded session gets wrong costs more than
the in-flight state it drops. A HEADLESS WORKER is never restarted or handed off from here — its loop already
gives it a fresh context — so it is told to journal and END ITS TURN, and one fixed line goes into the journal
saying so, which is what stops cc-loop reading an iteration that ended itself as a refusal (`worker`, `MARK`).
CC_ROLE=worker is the tell, not the tmux window: the loop HOLDS the track's window while the session inside it
is headless, and reading the window as live is what handed spend-tracker an interactive successor (2026-09-01).

THIS FILE MEASURES; IT DECIDES NO PHASES (review rec #8, 2026-08-31). Crossing a line emits ONE event —
`cc-handoff --event <target> --level …` — and cc-handoff, which owns the whole handoff lifecycle, decides what
it means: start a successor ALONGSIDE (CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP=1: it reads the journal, asks what the journal
missed, and takes over only when it says so), fall back to the stop-then-restart path above, or nothing. The
deadline of an open overlap is policed by the same event, from the predecessor's own Stop hook, which already
runs every turn — no new timer. What comes back is recorded here, and the session is told in the same reply.
Because an overlap interrupts nobody there is nothing to defer on, so the "PENDING, deferred again" loop above
cannot happen while it is on; `in_flight` is kept, demoted to the successor's opening checklist.

THE RECORD. Every crossing appends one JSON line to ~/.cc/state/context/handoffs.jsonl: when, which session,
repo/track, the percentage and the token count, and which line fired (journal · handoff · ceiling, the
`-deferred` forms when something was in flight, and the `-retry` forms for a second or later attempt). Countable with `wc -l`, so a reader never parses prose.

The message is delivered through the Stop hook's own reply channel: `{"decision":"block","reason":…}` makes
the session continue and do what the reason says (verified 2026-08-30 — it works headless too), and the
payload's `stop_hook_active` is what stops that from repeating. It is said once per line crossed, and only to
a session that HAS a journal to write (a track): an owner's conversation is not interrupted to write one.

WHY A TOOL AND NOT A GUESS. Handing off (`cc handoff`) costs a session its working memory, so it must happen
on evidence: too early wastes a good context, too late and auto-compaction has already thrown work away.
Nothing in the box knew the number — the decision was a human's hunch about how long a session had been up.

THE STOP HOOK CANNOT TELL YOU (verified empirically 2026-08-30, Claude Code 2.1.251, by wiring a hook that
dumps its stdin). The whole payload is: session_id, transcript_path, cwd, prompt_id, permission_mode,
hook_event_name, stop_hook_active, last_assistant_message, background_tasks, session_crons. No token counts,
no percentage. What it does give is `transcript_path`, and the transcript holds the truth.

USED = the last assistant entry's `message.usage`: input_tokens + cache_creation + cache_read. That IS the
prompt the model just saw, cache or not. If a `compact_boundary` follows it (Claude compacted after the last
answer) its `compactMetadata.postTokens` is the number instead — the old figure describes a context that no
longer exists. Sidechain (subagent) entries never count: they have their own window.

WINDOW is the part worth being honest about, because the transcript's `message.model` has the `[1m]` beta
suffix stripped — "claude-opus-5" alone cannot tell 200k from 1M, and getting that wrong reports 75% at 15%.
Sources, in order, first one wins, and the source is always printed:
  env         CC_CTX_WINDOW
  compacted   this session's own `compact_boundary.preTokens` — where IT actually hit the wall
  cost-state  the transcript's `cost-state.modelUsage` keys, the exact model id, suffix and all
  process     the live `claude` process's --model, matched by session id, or by cwd for a headless
              worker (`claude -p` is given no --session-id, so its id is nowhere on its command line)
  cc-model    the model the box currently starts sessions with, when it is the same family — it also RAISES a
              process/cost-state window: a headless worker is launched `--model claude-opus-5` with no suffix
              while the box runs that same family at 1M, and one box does not run one family at two sizes
  assumed     nothing said: 200k, flagged as an assumption in every output (never silently)
Then evidence overrides all of it: used above the window means the window is bigger, so it is raised.

Records: ~/.cc/state/context/<session-id>.json (the Stop hook writes one per turn; pruned after 7 days),
plus the append-only handoffs.jsonl above, which is never pruned.
Env: CC_CTX_WINDOW · CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL · CC_CTX_RECORDS (a test run keeps its records out of the box's).
"""
import glob, json, os, re, subprocess, sys, time

STATE = os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state")
RECORDS = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_RECORDS") or os.path.join(STATE, "context")
HANDOFF = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_DIR") or os.path.join(STATE, "handoff")
PROJECTS = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
DEFAULT_WINDOW = 200_000
BIG_WINDOW = 1_000_000
TAIL = 256 * 1024          # enough for many turns; grown when no assistant entry is in it
MAX_TAIL = 32 * 1024 * 1024
PRUNE_AFTER = 7 * 86400


def cfg(name, default):
    """A number from the environment, else ~/.cc/config — through cc-config, which applies that precedence
    for every tool on the box and never sources the file (it holds tokens)."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-config"), "get", name], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
        v = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        v = None
    try:
        return float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return default


def thresholds(window=BIG_WINDOW):
    """The three lines, in TOKENS, for a window: a percentage each, with a floor under the first two.
    Degradation tracks absolute tokens more than the fraction used, so a small window does not buy a session
    more good tokens — but a bare percentage of one lands under what a session needs to do anything at all
    (30% of 128k is 38k, most of it prompt and tools). Floors keep the rule 'as low as we can sit, never so
    low that a session is handed off before it can work'. The ceiling never sits under the handoff line."""
    j = max(window * cfg("CC_CONTEXT_WARN_PCT", 30.0) / 100, cfg("CC_CONTEXT_WARN_MIN", 60000))
    h = max(window * cfg("CC_CONTEXT_HANDOFF_PCT", 40.0) / 100, cfg("CC_CONTEXT_HANDOFF_MIN", 90000))
    return j, h, max(window * cfg("CC_CONTEXT_CEILING_PCT", 60.0) / 100, h)


def retry_lines(window=BIG_WINDOW):
    """The retry lines between the handoff line and the ceiling, in TOKENS: one every CC_CONTEXT_RETRY_PCT of
    the window (5 -> 45, 50, 55 on the defaults). A handoff that died leaves the session with nothing before
    the ceiling, which is what let one abandoned overlap park a session for the rest of its life. 0 = none."""
    _, h, c = thresholds(window)
    step = window * cfg("CC_CONTEXT_RETRY_PCT", 5.0) / 100
    if step <= 0:
        return []
    out, x = [], h + step
    while x < c and len(out) < 64:
        out.append(x)
        x += step
    return out


LEVELS = ["journal", "handoff", "ceiling"]


def level_of(used, window):
    j, h, c = thresholds(window)
    return "ceiling" if used >= c else ("handoff" if used >= h else ("journal" if used >= j else None))


def human(n):
    if n >= 1_000_000:
        return f"{n/1_000_000:.1f}M".replace(".0M", "M")
    if n >= 1000:
        return f"{round(n/1000)}k"
    return str(n)


def tail_lines(path, nbytes):
    """The last <=nbytes of a file as whole lines (a truncated first line is dropped)."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        size = f.seek(0, 2)
        start = max(0, size - nbytes)
        f.seek(start)
        buf = f.read()
    if start:
        buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)[1] if b"\n" in buf else b""
    return [l for l in buf.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n") if l.strip()]


def scan(lines):
    """Last assistant usage, last compact boundary, last cost-state, the session's cwd."""
    usage = model = None
    compact = cost_models = cwd = None
    seen_assistant_at = compact_at = -1
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if '"' not in line:
            continue
        try:
            d = json.loads(line)
        except Exception:
            continue                                   # a half-written last line is normal while a session runs
        if not isinstance(d, dict) or d.get("isSidechain"):
            continue
        cwd = d.get("cwd") or cwd
        t = d.get("type")
        if t == "assistant":
            m = d.get("message") or {}
            u = m.get("usage") or {}
            if u:
                usage, model, seen_assistant_at = u, m.get("model"), i
        elif t == "system" and d.get("subtype") == "compact_boundary":
            compact, compact_at = d.get("compactMetadata") or {}, i
        elif t == "cost-state":
            mu = d.get("modelUsage") or {}
            if mu:
                cost_models = list(mu)
    return usage, model, compact, compact_at, seen_assistant_at, cost_models, cwd


def window_from_model(model):
    return BIG_WINDOW if model and "[1m]" in model else DEFAULT_WINDOW


def family(model):
    m = re.search(r"fable|opus|sonnet|haiku", model or "", re.I)
    return m.group(0).lower() if m else None


def process_model(session_id, cwd=None):
    """The --model a live session was launched with — the only place the [1m] suffix survives.
    By session id when the command line carries one; otherwise by cwd, which is how a headless
    worker is found (it is given no --session-id at all). Ambiguous cwd -> no answer."""
    by_cwd = []
    for pid in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not pid.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as f:
                cmd = f.read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not cmd or "claude" not in os.path.basename(cmd[0]):
            continue
        model = None
        for i, a in enumerate(cmd):
            if a == "--model" and i + 1 < len(cmd):
                model = cmd[i + 1]
            elif a.startswith("--model="):
                model = a.split("=", 1)[1]
        if not model:
            continue                                   # says nothing about the window; and a `grep <sid>` is not a session
        if session_id and session_id in cmd:
            return model
        try:
            if cwd and os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd") == cwd:
                by_cwd.append(model)
        except OSError:
            continue
    return by_cwd[0] if len(set(by_cwd)) == 1 else None


def box_model():
    """What the box starts sessions with now: the model override, else the settings default.
    CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL overrides both (empty = don't ask the box; the selfcheck sets it)."""
    v = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL")
    if v is not None:
        return v or None
    try:
        m = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-model"), "current"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout.strip()
        if m:
            return m
    except Exception:
        pass
    try:
        with open(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/settings.json")) as f:
            return (json.load(f).get("model") or "").strip() or None
    except Exception:
        return None


def measure(path, session_id=None):
    """{used, window, pct, ...} for a transcript, or None when it holds no usable turn yet."""
    n = TAIL
    while True:
        lines = tail_lines(path, n)
        usage, model, compact, compact_at, assistant_at, cost_models, cwd = scan(lines)
        if usage or compact or n >= MAX_TAIL or n >= os.path.getsize(path):
            break
        n *= 8
    if not usage and not compact:
        return None
    used = 0
    if usage:
        used = (usage.get("input_tokens", 0) + usage.get("cache_creation_input_tokens", 0)
                + usage.get("cache_read_input_tokens", 0))
    compacted = False
    if compact and compact_at > assistant_at and compact.get("postTokens"):
        used, compacted = compact["postTokens"], True     # the pre-compact number describes a context that is gone

    window, src = None, None
    env = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_WINDOW")
    if env and env.isdigit():
        window, src = int(env), "env"
    if window is None and compact and compact.get("preTokens"):
        pre = compact["preTokens"]                        # where this very session hit the wall: the best evidence there is
        window, src = (BIG_WINDOW if pre > DEFAULT_WINDOW * 1.25 else DEFAULT_WINDOW), "compacted"
    if window is None and cost_models:
        window, src = max(window_from_model(m) for m in cost_models), "cost-state"
    if window is None:
        pm = process_model(session_id, cwd)
        if pm:
            window, src, model = window_from_model(pm), "process", pm
    if window is None:
        bm = box_model()
        if bm and family(bm) and family(bm) == family(model):   # same family, so the same window; a different one says nothing
            window, src, model = window_from_model(bm), "cc-model", bm
    if src in ("process", "cost-state"):
        bm = box_model()                                  # the box runs ONE model family, and it runs it at one size:
        if bm and family(bm) == family(model) and window_from_model(bm) > window:   # a headless worker is launched
            window, src = window_from_model(bm), src + "+cc-model"                  # `--model claude-opus-5`, no suffix
    if window is None:
        window, src = DEFAULT_WINDOW, "assumed"
    if used > window:                                     # evidence beats every assumption above it
        window, src = BIG_WINDOW if used <= BIG_WINDOW else used, src + "+observed"
    return {"used": used, "window": window, "pct": round(100.0 * used / window, 1),
            "model": model, "window_src": src, "compacted": compacted,
            "transcript": path, "session_id": session_id,
            "ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())}


# ---- the pane's own number ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# measure() above is an ESTIMATE: it sums the last turn's usage off the transcript and infers the window from
# whatever evidence it can find. The session itself knows both exactly, and /context prints them — so when the
# pane is IDLE we can simply ask it (owner, 2026-09-01: "a good way for the system to verify context for the
# sake of handoffs"). cc-msg --ask owns the keyboard rule: it refuses a busy pane and one holding a dialog, and
# it never spools, because a /context typed by some later drain answers to nobody.

PANE_TOKENS = re.compile(r"([\d.,]+)\s*([kKmM])?\s*/\s*([\d.,]+)\s*([kKmM])?\s*tokens\s*\((\d+(?:\.\d+)?)\s*%\)")


def _tokens(n, suffix):
    return int(float(n.replace(",", "")) * {"k": 1000, "m": 1_000_000}.get((suffix or "").lower(), 1))


def pane_ask(target, cmd="/context"):
    """What `cmd` draws in that target's pane, or None when the pane would not take it."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), "--ask", target, cmd],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=90)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() else None


def pane_measure(target):
    """{used, window, pct, ...} read off the session's OWN /context, or None when the pane did not answer.
    The LAST match wins: an older /context can still be scrolled up the pane, and the one just typed is at the
    bottom. The percentage is the session's own, not one recomputed here — asking it and then not believing it
    would be the estimate again with extra steps."""
    out = pane_ask(target)
    if not out:
        return None
    hits = PANE_TOKENS.findall(out)
    if not hits:
        return None
    used_n, used_s, win_n, win_s, pct = hits[-1]
    used, window = _tokens(used_n, used_s), _tokens(win_n, win_s)
    if window <= 0:
        return None
    return {"used": used, "window": window, "pct": float(pct),
            "model": None, "window_src": "pane", "compacted": False,
            "transcript": None, "session_id": None,
            "ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())}


# ---- finding a session's transcript ------------------------------------------------------------

def transcript_for_session(sid):
    hits = glob.glob(os.path.join(PROJECTS, "*", sid + ".jsonl"))
    return max(hits, key=os.path.getmtime) if hits else None


def transcript_for_cwd(cwd):
    d = os.path.join(PROJECTS, re.sub(r"[/.]", "-", os.path.realpath(cwd)))
    hits = [p for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.jsonl"))]
    return max(hits, key=os.path.getmtime) if hits else None


def board_get(repo, track, field):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board"), "get", repo, track, field],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        return r.stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        return ""


def resolve(repo=None, track=None, sid=None, cwd=None):
    """-> (transcript_path, session_id) or (None, None)."""
    if sid:
        return transcript_for_session(sid), sid
    if repo and track:
        sid = board_get(repo, track, "session_id")
        p = transcript_for_session(sid) if sid else None
        if not p:
            p = transcript_for_cwd(os.path.expanduser(f"~/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/{track}"))
        return p, (sid or None)
    p = transcript_for_cwd(cwd or os.getcwd())
    return p, (os.path.basename(p)[:-6] if p else None)


# ---- records -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def track_of(cwd):
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(cwd, ".cc/track")) as f:
            repo, track = [l.strip() for l in f.read().splitlines()[:2]]
        return repo, track
    except Exception:
        return None, None


def git(cwd, *args):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", cwd, *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None


def in_flight(payload, cwd):
    """Why this session must NOT be restarted right now, or None. Deliberately pessimistic: a wrong 'idle'
    reading is a handoff in the middle of something, and the state a session holds mid-task is in nobody's
    file. Anything git cannot answer counts as busy."""
    if payload.get("background_tasks"):
        return "a background task is still running"
    if payload.get("session_crons"):
        return "a scheduled task is pending"
    if payload.get("stop_hook_active"):
        return "another Stop hook is holding the session"
    top = git(cwd, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if not top:
        return None                                   # not a repo: git has nothing half-done to lose
    wts = git(cwd, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain")
    if wts is None:
        return "git could not be read"
    for l in wts.splitlines():                        # every worktree of this repo: a planning session's
        if not l.startswith("worktree "):             # own conflict resolution happens inside a track's
            continue
        w = l.split(" ", 1)[1]
        gd = git(w, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir")
        if gd is None:
            return f"git could not be read in {os.path.basename(w)}"
        for marker in ("MERGE_HEAD", "CHERRY_PICK_HEAD", "REVERT_HEAD", "BISECT_LOG", "rebase-merge", "rebase-apply"):
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(gd, marker)):
                return f"a merge/rebase is unfinished in {os.path.basename(w)}"
        st = git(w, "status", "--porcelain")
        if st is None:
            return f"git could not be read in {os.path.basename(w)}"
        if st:
            return f"uncommitted work in {os.path.basename(w)}"
        ahead = git(w, "rev-list", "--count", "@{upstream}..HEAD")
        if ahead and ahead != "0":
            return f"unpushed commits in {os.path.basename(w)}"
    return None


def ledger(m, kind, why=""):
    """One countable line per crossing — the App Home's handoff count is a `wc -l`, not a prose search."""
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    row = {"ts": m["ts"], "kind": kind, "session_id": m.get("session_id"), "repo": m.get("repo"),
           "track": m.get("track"), "pct": m["pct"], "used": m["used"], "window": m["window"],
           "model": m.get("model"), "cwd": m.get("cwd")}
    if why:
        row["why"] = why
    with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl"), "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")


def handoffs():
    """Every crossing ever recorded, oldest first. The App Home's count is len() of this."""
    out = []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl")) as f:
            for l in f:
                try:
                    out.append(json.loads(l))
                except Exception:
                    continue                       # one bad line never hides the rest
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def have_window(target):
    """Is there a live tmux window for this target? A headless worker has none — its loop is its handoff.
    By TARGET (`<repo>` or `<repo>/<track>`), not by repo+track: a planning session has a window too, and used
    to be told "there is no interactive session to restart here", which was simply untrue of it."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-windows", "-t", "main", "-F", "#W"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        return target in r.stdout.split()
    except Exception:
        return False


def overlap_open(target):
    """Is an overlapping handoff already open here? Only whether the record is THERE — cc-handoff owns its
    shape — so a turn does not fork a process for the sessions (nearly all of them) that have no overlap."""
    return os.path.exists(os.path.join(HANDOFF, target.replace("/", "--") + ".json"))


def worker():
    """A headless cc-loop iteration (CC_ROLE=worker, exported by cc-loop around its `claude -p`; a Stop hook
    inherits it). There is no interactive session here to restart, and none worth starting a successor beside:
    the loop's NEXT iteration, reading the journal, is the handoff. The tmux window is not the tell — the LOOP
    holds the track's window while the session inside it is headless, which is how spend-tracker's iteration
    read as live, opened an overlap, and left an interactive successor sitting in the track window long after
    the loop was done (2026-09-01). cc-handoff refuses the same way; this is the half that decides what the
    session is TOLD."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_ROLE") == "worker"


MARK = "- cc-context: iteration stopped at "     # cc-loop greps this prefix (`ctx_stopped`) — the contract
                                                 # between the two files, and the reason it is a fixed string.


def mark_journal(m, lvl):
    """A worker told to hand off ends its turn — write that in the JOURNAL, as a line nothing has to interpret.
    cc-loop reads it and carries straight on to the next iteration: an iteration that ended ITSELF is neither a
    refusal nor a reroute, and rerunning it would redo work the next fresh context can already read (the loop
    misread exactly that on spend-tracker). Written here rather than asked of the model, because a marker a
    model paraphrases is not a marker. Never raises: a note that cannot be written must not break a session."""
    try:
        pth = journal_path(m)
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(pth), exist_ok=True)
        with open(pth, "a") as f:
            # unique per iteration ON PURPOSE: cc-loop's journal diff drops a line byte-identical to an earlier one,
            # and two stops at the same rounded percentage would otherwise write the same line (review of #164).
            who = f"{m.get('ts') or ''}, session {(m.get('session_id') or '?')[:8]}"
            f.write(f"{MARK}{m['pct']:.0f}% of its context window ({lvl}, {who}) — it journalled and ended its turn; "
                    f"the next iteration continues from this journal.\n")
    except Exception:
        pass


def mid_handoff():
    """Is this session the second half of a handoff that is STILL OPEN? $CC_HANDOFF is exported into a
    successor by `cc __runnext` and outlives the handoff itself — it says "born a successor", not "still
    being handed off". Read as a bare flag it silences this file for the REST of that session's life: on
    2026-09-01 the planning session won an overlap in the morning and then sat past the hand-off line all
    evening at 51.5% with no event ever emitted. <id>.done is the marker cc-guard already un-gates on; see
    cc-handoff.mid_handoff for the long version. Unaccountable id -> still held: a session must never police
    the handoff it is the second half of, and every failure here falls that way."""
    hid = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF") or ""
    if not hid:
        return False
    return not (re.match(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$", hid)
                and os.path.exists(os.path.join(HANDOFF, hid + ".done")))


def event(target, m, level, busy="", live=False, taken=False, retry=False):
    """THE ONE CALL OUT. This file measures a context and says which LINE was crossed; cc-handoff owns every
    phase that follows — the deadline of an overlap already open, whether this crossing starts one, whether it
    falls back to the stop-then-restart path, and the sweep for records no session is left to finish. Before
    review rec #8 that decision was made here, from a third file's building blocks, and `cc` held a fourth
    piece of it. The level always goes out, even when this session can take no more phases (`taken`), because
    the watchdog for an overlap already open decides on it: at the ceiling it FORCES the cutover, below it
    abandons. -> the action cc-handoff took: "overlap", "replace", or "" (nothing).
    Answered on stdout as JSON so a phase's own words cannot be mistaken for the answer; anything unreadable
    reads as "" — the session is then simply TOLD, which is the direction every failure here must fall."""
    argv = [os.path.join(BIN, "cc-handoff"), "--event", target, "--level", level or "",
            "--pct", f"{m['pct']:.1f}"]
    if taken:
        argv += ["--taken"]                # this session has already taken a phase, or a Stop hook is holding
                                           # it: the watchdog still runs on the level, nothing new starts
    if m.get("session_id"):
        argv += ["--session", m["session_id"]]
    if busy:
        argv += ["--in-flight", busy]      # what a journal always omits: what is unfinished RIGHT NOW
    if live:
        argv += ["--live"]                 # no live tmux window = a headless worker: its loop is its handoff
    if retry:
        argv += ["--retry"]                # a line above the handoff line: the attempt already taken may be dead
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=90)
        return (json.loads(r.stdout or "{}") or {}).get("action") or ""
    except Exception:
        return ""


def journal_of(m):
    return (f"~/.cc/state/{m['repo']}/{m['track']}/progress.md" if m.get("track")
            else f"~/.cc/state/{m['repo']}/planning-handoff.md")


def journal_path(m):
    """The same file journal_of() NAMES, as a path something can open. Built from STATE so a selfcheck writes
    its fixtures into a temp dir and never into the box's real journals."""
    return (os.path.join(STATE, m["repo"], m["track"], "progress.md") if m.get("track")
            else os.path.join(STATE, m["repo"], "planning-handoff.md"))


def told(m, lvl, deferred="", restartable=True, overlapped=False, ending=False):
    """What the session is told when it crosses a line — the Stop hook's reason."""
    s = (f"CONTEXT {m['pct']:.0f}% ({human(m['used'])} of {human(m['window'])}). Append a dated handoff entry to "
         f"{journal_of(m)} now, while there is still room to write a good one: what is done, what is next "
         f"(ordered), gotchas. Write it from what you already know — do not go looking, and a line or two is "
         f"enough if that is all there is. A fresh session starts from that file and from nothing else.")
    if lvl == "journal":
        return s + " Then carry on."
    if overlapped:
        return s + (" Your SUCCESSOR is already running alongside you, in this same directory, and it is not "
                    "live yet: it has the box's own packet (`cc-handoff --brief`) and will message you ONCE for "
                    "what no file holds. Answer that batch in ONE turn — a turn of yours costs a whole context, "
                    "and eighteen of them is what the last handover cost — then carry on: nothing is being "
                    "restarted underneath you, and it takes over only when it says so.")
    # `ending` is the WORKER's branch and only the worker's, before `deferred`: a worker with anything
    # uncommitted was told a handoff was PENDING and to carry on — the one thing it must not do at its ceiling.
    # Gated on `not restartable` it also caught planning sessions and windowless interactive ones, telling them
    # a loop that does not exist would continue them (review of #164). What is in flight goes into the entry.
    if ending:
        return s + (f" Put what is unfinished right now into it — {deferred}." if deferred else "") + \
            (" Then END YOUR TURN — do not start anything new, and do not hand off: there is no interactive "
             "session to restart here, so this entry IS the handoff. The loop begins the next iteration from "
             "it and from nothing else.")
    if deferred:
        return s + (f" A handoff is now PENDING: it happens at the first turn with nothing in flight, and right "
                    f"now {deferred}. Finish that; nothing is being restarted underneath you.")
    if not restartable:
        return s + (" There is no interactive session to restart here, so this entry IS the handoff — the next "
                    "session starts from it and from nothing else.")
    return s + " This session is being handed off now."


def record(payload):
    """Called by the Stop hook. Never raises: a measurement must not be able to break a session."""
    sid = payload.get("session_id") or ""
    path = payload.get("transcript_path") or ""
    cwd = payload.get("cwd") or ""
    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
        path = transcript_for_session(sid) if sid else None
    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
        return None
    m = measure(path, sid)
    if not m:
        return None
    root = git(cwd, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel") if cwd else ""
    repo, track = track_of(root or cwd or "")
    if not repo and cwd and os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(cwd)) == os.path.expanduser("~/dev"):
        repo = os.path.basename(os.path.realpath(cwd))   # a planning session: ~/dev/<repo>, no track marker
    m.update({"cwd": cwd, "repo": repo, "track": track, "level": level_of(m["used"], m["window"])})
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    p = os.path.join(RECORDS, (sid or os.path.basename(path)[:-6]) + ".json")
    was = {}
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            was = json.load(f) or {}
    except Exception:
        pass
    m["told"] = was.get("told")
    m["handed_off"] = was.get("handed_off")
    lvl = m["level"]
    # Only a session with a journal to write is ever interrupted — an owner's conversation is not.
    # never twice: once a handoff is taken, this session is being replaced and has nothing left to be told
    target = f"{repo}/{track}" if track else repo
    # no window = a headless worker: its loop is its handoff. So is a cc-loop ITERATION that happens to have
    # one — the loop occupies the track's window while the session inside it is headless (worker).
    live = bool(repo) and have_window(target) and not worker()
    # Anything the crossing MEANS is cc-handoff's to decide (review rec #8) — including the deadline of an
    # overlap already open, which is why this fires with no level too. Never from the successor of an OPEN
    # handoff: a session must not police, abandon or restart the handoff it is itself the second half of. Only
    # while it is open, though — once it retires the successor is the only session left and must hand ITSELF
    # off in turn (mid_handoff; reading $CC_HANDOFF as a bare flag is what stopped that all of 2026-09-01).
    # cc-handoff refuses the same way — this is the cheap half of the rule, so the one session in a hundred
    # that IS mid-handoff does not fork a process every turn to be told no.
    act = ""
    taken = bool(m["handed_off"]) or bool(payload.get("stop_hook_active"))
    fresh = bool(lvl) and bool(repo) and not taken
    # A RETRY LINE. Every five percent between the handoff line and the ceiling — and the ceiling itself, last
    # of them — is one more crossing for a session whose handoff was TAKEN and then died: without it
    # `handed_off` read as "already taken" for the rest of that session's life, which is how the overlap
    # started at 01:35Z on 2026-09-05 and later abandoned left the box with nothing to try. What is remembered
    # is the highest line already crossed IN TOKENS, with the window it was measured against: each line fires
    # once, a turn between two of them fires nothing, and a WINDOW CORRECTION — 150k read as 75% of an assumed
    # 200k, then the evidence says 1M — re-keys the lines rather than carrying a stale reading across them,
    # which would leave the ceiling as the only line again. Whether a crossing MEANS a new attempt is
    # cc-handoff's call.
    lines = retry_lines(m["window"]) + [thresholds(m["window"])[2]]
    crossed = max([x for x in lines if m["used"] >= x], default=0)
    was_crossed = (was.get("retried") or 0) if was.get("retried_window") == m["window"] else 0
    m["retried"], m["retried_window"] = max(crossed, was_crossed), m["window"]
    retry = bool(lvl in ("handoff", "ceiling") and repo and m["handed_off"] and crossed > was_crossed
                 and not payload.get("stop_hook_active") and not overlap_open(target))
    busy = in_flight(payload, cwd) if ((fresh or retry) and lvl != "ceiling") else None   # the ceiling goes regardless
    # …and never from a worker either: cc-handoff refuses it a phase (its `worker`), so forking that process
    # every turn, for what is a large share of this box's sessions, only buys a "no". Same cheap half of the
    # rule as mid_handoff above — the authoritative half stays over there.
    if repo and not mid_handoff() and not worker() and ((fresh and lvl != "journal") or retry
                                                       or overlap_open(target)):
        act = event(target, m, lvl or "", busy or "", live, taken, retry)
    if fresh or retry:
        kind = (lvl + "-retry") if (retry and not fresh) else lvl
        if act == "overlap":                           # a successor is coming up alongside: nothing is restarted
            m["handed_off"] = m["ts"]
            m["told"] = lvl
            m["say"] = told(m, lvl, overlapped=True)
            ledger(m, kind)
        elif act == "replace":                         # the old path: it will ask this session to journal and exit
            m["handed_off"] = m["ts"]
            ledger(m, kind)
        elif fresh and (LEVELS.index(lvl) > LEVELS.index(was.get("told") or LEVELS[0]) or not was.get("told")):
            restartable = bool(live) and bool(track)   # only a track has the stop-then-restart path
            m["told"] = lvl                            # said once per line crossed, not once per turn
            m["say"] = told(m, lvl, busy or "", restartable, ending=worker())
            ledger(m, lvl if lvl == "journal" else lvl + "-deferred",
                   busy or ("no live session to restart" if not restartable else ""))
            if worker() and track and lvl != "journal":
                mark_journal(m, lvl)               # …and the loop reads that line instead of classifying the run
    m["pending"] = bool(lvl and lvl != "journal" and not m["handed_off"])
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(m, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    now = time.time()
    for old in glob.glob(os.path.join(RECORDS, "*.json")):
        try:
            if now - os.path.getmtime(old) > PRUNE_AFTER:
                os.remove(old)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return m


def records():
    out = []
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(RECORDS, "*.json"))):
        try:
            with open(p) as f:
                d = json.load(f)
            d["age"] = int(time.time() - os.path.getmtime(p))
            out.append(d)
        except Exception:
            continue
    out.sort(key=lambda d: d["age"])
    return out


def newest_per_track(rs):
    """A track keeps its name across handoffs but not its session id: newest record wins."""
    best = {}
    for d in rs:
        if not d.get("repo") or not d.get("track"):
            continue
        k = (d["repo"], d["track"])
        if k not in best:                                # records() is already newest-first
            best[k] = d
    return best


# ---- output ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def line(m):
    s = f"{m['pct']:.0f}%  {human(m['used'])}/{human(m['window'])}"
    if m.get("model"):
        s += f"  {m['model']}"
    s += f"  window from {m['window_src']}"
    if m["window_src"] == "assumed":
        s += " — NO evidence for the window, so the % may be wrong"
    if m.get("compacted"):
        s += "  (just compacted)"
    lvl = m.get("level") or level_of(m["used"], m["window"])
    if lvl:
        s += {"journal": "  — journal a handoff", "handoff": "  — hand off",
              "ceiling": "  — hand off NOW (ceiling)"}[lvl]
    if m.get("pending"):
        s += "  (handoff pending)"
    return s


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["selfcheck"]:
        return selfcheck()
    as_json = quiet = do_all = do_record = do_handoffs = do_true = False
    over = None
    path = sid = repo = track = None
    args = []
    while argv:
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--json":
            as_json = True
        elif a in ("-q", "--quiet"):
            quiet = True
        elif a == "--all":
            do_all = True
        elif a == "--thresholds":
            print(" ".join(f"{cfg(k, d):g}" for k, d in (("CC_CONTEXT_WARN_PCT", 30.0),
                                                         ("CC_CONTEXT_HANDOFF_PCT", 40.0),
                                                         ("CC_CONTEXT_CEILING_PCT", 60.0))))
            return 0
        elif a == "--record":
            do_record = True
        elif a == "--handoffs":
            do_handoffs = True
        elif a == "--true":
            do_true = True
        elif a == "--over":
            over = float(argv.pop(0)) if argv and not argv[0].startswith("-") and argv[0][:1].isdigit() \
                else "level"                      # bare --over = the handoff line for THAT session's window
        elif a == "--transcript":
            path = argv.pop(0)
        elif a == "--session":
            sid = argv.pop(0)
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            print(f"cc-context: unknown option {a}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        else:
            args.append(a)
    if len(args) >= 2:
        repo, track = args[0], args[1]

    if do_record:
        try:
            payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
            m = record(payload if isinstance(payload, dict) else {})
            if m and m.get("say"):   # the Stop hook's own reply channel: the session continues and does this
                print(json.dumps({"decision": "block", "reason": m["say"]}))
        except Exception:
            pass
        return 0

    if do_handoffs:
        hs = handoffs()
        if as_json:
            print(json.dumps(hs, indent=2))
            return 0
        for h in hs:
            print(f"{h.get('ts')}\t{h.get('kind')}\t{(h.get('repo') or '-')}/{(h.get('track') or '-')}"
                  f"\t{h.get('pct')}\t{h.get('used')}\t{h.get('window')}\t{h.get('session_id') or '-'}")
        return 0

    if do_all:
        rs = records()
        if as_json:
            print(json.dumps(rs, indent=2))
            return 0
        for d in rs:
            print(f"{d.get('repo') or '-'}\t{d.get('track') or '-'}\t{d['pct']:.0f}\t{d['used']}"
                  f"\t{d['window']}\t{d['window_src']}\t{d['age']}\t{d.get('level') or '-'}"
                  f"\t{'pending' if d.get('pending') else '-'}")
        return 0

    m = None
    if do_true:
        # A TARGET, ALWAYS: --true types at a keyboard, and the one keyboard it must never type at is its own
        # caller's. `<repo> <track>` and a bare `<target>` (`box`, `repo/track`) are the same tmux window name.
        target = f"{repo}/{track}" if repo and track else (args[0] if args else None)
        if not target:
            print("cc-context: --true needs a target — the tmux window to ask (<repo> <track>, or box)",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        m = pane_measure(target)          # None = the pane was busy, in a dialog, or gone: fall back to the estimate
        if not m and not path and not sid and not (repo and track):
            # …TO THE TARGET'S OWN ESTIMATE, never the caller's. `--true repo/track` names the session in one
            # word, so split it back into the pair resolve() wants; with nothing to go on resolve() returns the
            # newest transcript for THIS process's cwd, which is the session that typed the command — a
            # different number, a different level, and a different --over answer, reported as if it were the
            # target's. A target with no slash (`box`) names no board row, so there is nothing to fall back to.
            if "/" in target:
                repo, track = target.split("/", 1)
            else:
                if not quiet:
                    print(f"cc-context: {target}'s pane would not answer, and `{target}` is not a <repo>/<track> "
                          "to estimate from", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
    if not m:
        if not path:
            path, sid = resolve(repo, track, sid)
        if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
            if not quiet:
                what = f"{repo}/{track}" if repo else (sid or os.getcwd())
                print(f"cc-context: no transcript for {what}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        m = measure(path, sid)
        if not m:
            if not quiet:
                print(f"cc-context: {path} has no answered turn yet", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(m, indent=2))
    elif not quiet:
        print(line(m))
    if over is not None:
        if over == "level":
            return 0 if (m.get("level") or level_of(m["used"], m["window"])) in ("handoff", "ceiling") else 1
        return 0 if m["pct"] >= over else 1
    return 0


# ---- selfcheck ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def selfcheck():
    import tempfile
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  FAIL {name}")

    def tx(entries):
        fd, path = tempfile.mkstemp(suffix=".jsonl")
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
            for e in entries:
                fh.write(json.dumps(e) + "\n")
        return path

    def asst(model, inp, cc, cr, out=10, side=False):
        return {"type": "assistant", "isSidechain": side, "message": {
            "model": model, "usage": {"input_tokens": inp, "cache_creation_input_tokens": cc,
                                      "cache_read_input_tokens": cr, "output_tokens": out}}}

    env0 = os.environ.pop("CC_CTX_WINDOW", None)
    box0 = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL")
    os.environ["CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL"] = ""        # hermetic: what THIS box runs must not decide a fixture's window
    role0 = os.environ.pop("CC_ROLE", None)    # a --go worker runs these gates as often as a session does, and
                                               # every case below would take the worker branch if its own role
                                               # leaked in. Same trap as $CC_HANDOFF, restored at the end.
    hnd0 = os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)  # …and WHO IS RUNNING THE TEST must not decide it either. The overlap
                                               # cases below are the only ones that read this, and they read it off
                                               # the ambient environment: `not os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF")` is how a
                                               # successor is stopped from policing the handoff it is half of. So a
                                               # selfcheck run BY a successor silently took that branch, no overlap
                                               # ever started, and the run died on an empty list — 2026-08-31, which
                                               # is how a green main looked like four red cases and blocked every
                                               # landing that session could attempt. A gate that fails on who is
                                               # holding it is not a gate.

    # used = input + cache_creation + cache_read of the LAST assistant turn
    t = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 5, 100, 200), asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000)])
    m = measure(t)
    ok("used = last turn's input+cache_creation+cache_read", m["used"] == 10010)
    ok("assumed window is flagged", m["window"] == DEFAULT_WINDOW and m["window_src"] == "assumed")
    ok("assumption is stated in the output", "NO evidence" in line(m))

    # a subagent's window is its own: sidechain entries never count
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000), asst("claude-opus-5", 0, 0, 500000, side=True)]))
    ok("sidechain entries are ignored", m["used"] == 10010)

    # cost-state carries the exact model id, [1m] and all
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000),
                    {"type": "cost-state", "modelUsage": {"claude-opus-5[1m]": {"inputTokens": 1}}}]))
    ok("cost-state gives the 1M window", m["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and m["window_src"] == "cost-state")
    ok("pct is against the real window", m["pct"] == 1.0)

    # a compact boundary AFTER the last answer replaces the number and dates the window
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 989000),
                    {"type": "system", "subtype": "compact_boundary",
                     "compactMetadata": {"trigger": "auto", "preTokens": 1000830, "postTokens": 14226}}]))
    ok("post-compact used = postTokens", m["used"] == 14226 and m["compacted"])
    ok("the session's own compaction sizes the window", m["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and m["window_src"] == "compacted")

    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-sonnet-5", 10, 1000, 189000),
                    {"type": "system", "subtype": "compact_boundary",
                     "compactMetadata": {"preTokens": 190000, "postTokens": 9000}}]))
    ok("compacting at 190k means a 200k window", m["window"] == DEFAULT_WINDOW)

    # never a percentage above 100: evidence beats the assumption
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 0, 0, 640000)]))
    ok("used above the assumed window raises it", m["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and m["pct"] == 64.0)
    ok("the override is visible in the source", m["window_src"] == "assumed+observed")

    # the box's own model answers only for its own family: opus-5 and opus-5[1m] are one model, fable is not
    os.environ["CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL"] = "claude-opus-5[1m]"
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000)]))
    ok("cc-model's [1m] sizes a same-family session", m["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and m["window_src"] == "cc-model")
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-sonnet-5", 10, 1000, 9000)]))
    ok("a different family says nothing", m["window_src"] == "assumed")
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000),
                    {"type": "cost-state", "modelUsage": {"claude-opus-5": {"inputTokens": 1}}}]))
    ok("a suffix-less id is raised to the size the box runs that family at",
       m["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and m["window_src"] == "cost-state+cc-model")
    os.environ["CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL"] = ""

    os.environ["CC_CTX_WINDOW"] = "500000"
    m = measure(tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 0, 0, 250000)]))
    ok("CC_CTX_WINDOW wins", m["window"] == 500000 and m["pct"] == 50.0 and m["window_src"] == "env")
    del os.environ["CC_CTX_WINDOW"]

    # a transcript with nothing answered yet, and a half-written last line (a live session)
    ok("no answered turn -> None", measure(tx([{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user"}}])) is None)
    t = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000)])
    with open(t, "a") as fh:
        fh.write('{"type":"assistant","message":{"usa')
    ok("a half-written last line is skipped", measure(t)["used"] == 10010)

    # the tail is grown until a turn is found: a big transcript whose last turns are far back
    big = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 1, 2, 3)] + [{"type": "user", "pad": "x" * 4000} for _ in range(200)])
    ok("tail grows past 256k of padding", measure(big)["used"] == 6)

    # records: written from a Stop payload, read back newest-first per track
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
        global RECORDS, HANDOFF, STATE
        keeps, STATE = STATE, os.path.join(d, "state")     # mark_journal writes a real file: not the box's
        keep, RECORDS = RECORDS, os.path.join(d, "context")
        keeph, HANDOFF = HANDOFF, os.path.join(d, "handoff")   # mid_handoff() reads <id>.done from here: a
        os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)                    # fixture must never write into the box's own
        t = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 10, 1000, 9000)])
        r = record({"session_id": "sid-1", "transcript_path": t, "cwd": d})
        ok("record writes a measurement", r and r["used"] == 10010)
        ok("record file is keyed by session id", os.path.exists(os.path.join(RECORDS, "sid-1.json")))
        ok("a payload with no transcript records nothing", record({"session_id": "x"}) is None)
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, "wt/.cc"), exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(d, "wt/.cc/track"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("repo1\ntrack1\n")
        record({"session_id": "sid-2", "transcript_path": t, "cwd": os.path.join(d, "wt")})
        rs = records()
        ok("--all lists both records", len(rs) == 2)
        best = newest_per_track(rs)
        ok("repo/track comes off the .cc/track marker", ("repo1", "track1") in best)

        # the three lines: a percentage each, with a floor under the first two
        ok("30/40/60 of the 1M window", thresholds(BIG_WINDOW) == (300000.0, 400000.0, 600000.0))
        ok("the floors hold a 200k window up", thresholds(DEFAULT_WINDOW) == (60000.0, 90000.0, 120000.0))
        ok("on 128k the floor is the whole rule", thresholds(128000) == (60000.0, 90000.0, 90000.0))
        ok("the ceiling is never under the handoff", thresholds(100000)[2] >= thresholds(100000)[1])
        ok("levels, in order", [level_of(u, BIG_WINDOW) for u in (299999, 300000, 400000, 600000)]
                               == [None, "journal", "handoff", "ceiling"])
        ok("the same rule fires on a 200k model", level_of(60000, DEFAULT_WINDOW) == "journal")

        # nothing real is ever restarted or started from a selfcheck: the ONE shell-out is stubbed. It stands
        # in for cc-handoff, which owns the phases — so this fixture answers exactly as that CLI does, with the
        # action it took, and these cases assert what THIS file does with the answer, never what cc-handoff
        # decides (that has its own selfcheck, and selftest.sh drives the two together on real windows).
        global have_window, overlap_open, event
        hw0, ev0, oo0 = have_window, event, overlap_open
        live, events, started, on, tried = [True], [], [], [False], []
        have_window = lambda t: live[0]

        def event(t, m, level, busy="", live_=False, taken=False, retry=False):   # noqa: F811 — the stub, restored below
            events.append((t, level, busy, live_, taken, retry))
            if not level or level == "journal" or not live_ or any(x[0] == t for x in started):
                return ""
            if taken and not (retry and any(x == t for x in tried)):
                return ""                        # cc-handoff's `retryable`: only an attempt that DIED is remade
            if on[0]:
                started.append((t, busy))
                tried.append(t)
                return "overlap"
            return "replace" if "/" in t and not busy else ""

        def overlap_open(t):                 # noqa: F811
            return on[0] and any(x[0] == t for x in started)

        wt = os.path.join(d, "wt")
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", wt], check=False)      # a real repo: in_flight reads git, not a flag

        def at(used, sid, **extra):
            t2 = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 0, 0, used),
                     {"type": "cost-state", "modelUsage": {"claude-opus-5[1m]": {"inputTokens": 1}}}])
            return record({"session_id": sid, "transcript_path": t2, "cwd": wt, **extra})

        r = at(320000, "sid-3")
        ok("crossing the journal line tells the session", r["level"] == "journal" and "handoff entry" in r["say"])
        ok("the message names the journal, not the tool", "repo1/track1/progress.md" in r["say"])
        ok("the same line is not said twice", not at(320000, "sid-3").get("say"))

        # the handoff line with work in flight: PENDING, and nothing is restarted underneath the session
        r = at(420000, "sid-3")
        ok("in flight -> the handoff is pending, not taken", r["pending"] and not r["handed_off"]
           and events[-1][1:3] == ("handoff", in_flight({}, wt)))
        ok("the session is told what it is waiting on", "PENDING" in r["say"] and "uncommitted work" in r["say"])
        n0 = len([e for e in events if e[1] == "handoff" and not e[2]])
        r = at(420000, "sid-3", background_tasks=[{"id": "1"}])
        ok("a background task counts as in flight", r["pending"] and not r["handed_off"]
           and len([e for e in events if e[1] == "handoff" and not e[2]]) == n0)

        # the ceiling goes regardless: past it, a degraded session costs more than the state it drops
        r = at(650000, "sid-3")
        ok("the ceiling hands off with work still in flight", r["level"] == "ceiling" and r["handed_off"]
           and events[-1][1:3] == ("ceiling", ""))
        n1 = len(events)
        ok("a handoff already taken is never taken twice", at(650000, "sid-3")["handed_off"] == r["handed_off"]
           and len(events) == n1)                       # and with nothing open to watch, not even a fork goes out

        # nothing in flight: the pending handoff is taken at the next turn
        for a in (["add", "-A"], ["-c", "user.email=t@t", "-c", "user.name=t", "commit", "-qm", "x"]):
            subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, *a], check=False, capture_output=True)
        ok("a committed worktree is not in flight", in_flight({}, wt) is None)
        r = at(420000, "sid-6")
        ok("idle -> the handoff is executed", r["handed_off"] and events[-1][1:3] == ("handoff", ""))

        # a headless worker has no window to restart; its loop is the handoff, so it is told to journal
        live[0] = False
        r = at(420000, "sid-7")
        ok("no live session -> told, never restarted", not r["handed_off"] and "IS the handoff" in r["say"]
           and events[-1][3] is False)                  # the fact goes out with the event; the phase is not ours
        live[0] = True

        n3 = len(events)
        r = at(420000, "sid-8", stop_hook_active=True)
        ok("never while a Stop hook is already holding the session", "say" not in r
           and not r["handed_off"] and len(events) == n3)   # held = taken: with nothing open, not even a fork
        t9 = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 0, 0, 470000),
                 {"type": "cost-state", "modelUsage": {"claude-opus-5[1m]": {"inputTokens": 1}}}])
        n2 = len(events)
        r = record({"session_id": "sid-9", "transcript_path": t9, "cwd": d})
        ok("a session with no journal is not interrupted", r["level"] == "handoff" and "say" not in r
           and len(events) == n2)                       # …and nothing outside this file was even asked

        # the durable record: one countable line per crossing, no prose to parse
        hs = handoffs()
        ok("every crossing is on the ledger, and only crossings", len(hs) == 5)
        ok("the ledger says which line fired", [h["kind"] for h in hs]
           == ["journal", "handoff-deferred", "ceiling", "handoff", "handoff-deferred"])
        ok("a ledger line carries when/who/how full", all(h.get("ts") and h.get("pct") and h.get("used")
                                                          and h.get("window") and h.get("session_id") for h in hs))
        ok("repo and track are on it, so a count can be per track",
           all(h["repo"] == "repo1" and h["track"] == "track1" for h in hs))
        ok("a deferred line says what it was waiting on", any("uncommitted" in (h.get("why") or "") for h in hs))
        ok("the ledger outlives the per-session records", os.path.exists(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl")))

        # ---- the overlapping handoff: the successor starts ALONGSIDE, so nothing has to be idle ----
        ok("these cases read $CC_HANDOFF off the environment, so the caller's own must not be in it — a "
           "successor running the gates would otherwise see every case below take the successor branch",
           "CC_HANDOFF" not in os.environ)
        on[0] = True
        subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, "worktree", "list"], check=False, capture_output=True)
        open(os.path.join(wt, "dirty.txt"), "w").write("x")     # in flight again: today this deferred forever
        ok("work in flight still defers the OLD path", in_flight({}, wt))
        r = at(420000, "sid-10")
        ok("the overlap starts with work in flight — nothing to defer on",
           r["handed_off"] and started == [("repo1/track1", in_flight({}, wt))])
        ok("the session is told its successor is alive, not that it is being restarted",
           "SUCCESSOR" in r["say"] and "not live yet" in r["say"] and "PENDING" not in r["say"])
        ok("in_flight is demoted to the successor's opening checklist", "uncommitted work" in started[0][1])
        r2 = at(430000, "sid-10")
        ok("an overlap is never started twice for one target", len(started) == 1 and not r2.get("say"))
        # the deadline of an open overlap is the same event, with no level: this file has nothing to say about
        # a turn that crossed nothing, but cc-handoff still has a watchdog to run on it
        ok("an open overlap is handed the turn even when no line was crossed",
           at(100000, "sid-10") and events[-1][:2] == ("repo1/track1", ""))
        ok("...and once a phase is taken the MEASUREMENT still goes out, flagged, so the watchdog can decide "
           "on it: at the ceiling it forces the cutover instead of abandoning",
           at(650000, "sid-10") and events[-1][:2] == ("repo1/track1", "ceiling") and events[-1][4] is True)

        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = "x"       # the SUCCESSOR shares this cwd: it must not police its own handoff
        n = len(events)
        at(420000, "sid-11")
        ok("the successor never even emits the event for a handoff it is half of",
           len(events) == n and len(started) == 1)
        # …but only while that handoff is OPEN. $CC_HANDOFF lives as long as the successor's process, so read
        # as a bare flag it silenced this file for the rest of that session's life and the box could overlap a
        # target exactly once, ever — 2026-09-01, the planning session sat past the hand-off line all evening
        # at 51.5% and the handoff had to be started by hand. <id>.done is the difference (cc-guard:84).
        open(os.path.join(HANDOFF, "x.done"), "w").write("{}")
        started.clear()                      # …which is also what retiring did: the record was cleared with it
        n = len(events)
        r = at(420000, "sid-12")
        ok("a session whose own handoff has RETIRED hands off in its turn — no overlap is a one-shot",
           len(events) == n + 1 and len(started) == 1 and r["handed_off"] and "SUCCESSOR" in r["say"])
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = "../../etc/passwd"
        n, ns = len(events), len(started)
        at(430000, "sid-13")
        ok("a $CC_HANDOFF we cannot account for still holds — failure falls towards not policing your own",
           len(events) == n and len(started) == ns)
        os.environ.pop("CC_HANDOFF", None)

        # ---- a retry line. An overlap can DIE — the one at 01:35Z on 2026-09-05 sat on a permission prompt
        # and was abandoned — and `handed_off` then read as "already taken" for the rest of that session's
        # life: no event, at any percentage, ever again. Every five percent above the handoff line is now one
        # more crossing, and the ceiling is the last of them.
        ok("the retry lines sit between the handoff line and the ceiling",
           retry_lines(BIG_WINDOW) == [450000, 500000, 550000])
        ok("...on a small window they follow the floors, not the raw percentages",
           retry_lines(DEFAULT_WINDOW) == [100000.0, 110000.0])
        os.environ["CC_CONTEXT_RETRY_PCT"] = "0"
        ok("...and the step turns them off", retry_lines(BIG_WINDOW) == [])
        os.environ.pop("CC_CONTEXT_RETRY_PCT")
        started.clear()
        r = at(420000, "sid-r")                        # 42%: the handoff line, unchanged
        ok("the first crossing is the 40% one, exactly as before",
           r["handed_off"] and len(started) == 1 and events[-1][5] is False)
        n = len(events)
        r = at(430000, "sid-r")
        ok("a turn between two lines crosses nothing new", len(events) == n + 1
           and events[-1][5] is False and len(started) == 1)
        started.clear()                                # --abandon: the record is gone, the predecessor carries on
        n = len(events)
        at(435000, "sid-r")
        ok("with the overlap gone and no line crossed, nothing goes out at all", len(events) == n)
        r = at(455000, "sid-r")
        ok("the 45% line retries: a new overlap, and the session hears about that one too",
           events[-1][5] is True and len(started) == 1 and "SUCCESSOR" in r["say"])
        r2 = at(465000, "sid-r")
        ok("a crossing while an overlap is open starts nothing", len(started) == 1 and not r2.get("say")
           and events[-1][5] is False)
        started.clear()
        r = at(505000, "sid-r")
        ok("...and 50 does the same again if that attempt dies too",
           events[-1][5] is True and len(started) == 1 and r["handed_off"])
        started.clear()
        r = at(555000, "sid-r")
        ok("...and 55", events[-1][5] is True and len(started) == 1)
        started.clear()
        n, ns = len(events), len(started)
        at(560000, "sid-r")
        ok("there is no line between 55% and the ceiling", len(events) == n and len(started) == ns)
        r = at(605000, "sid-r")
        ok("the ceiling is the LAST of the lines: a dead overlap does not take its teeth with it",
           events[-1][1] == "ceiling" and events[-1][5] is True and len(started) == 1)
        ok("each attempt is its own line on the ledger",
           len([h for h in handoffs() if h.get("kind") == "handoff-retry"]) == 3
           and len([h for h in handoffs() if h.get("kind") == "ceiling-retry"]) == 1)
        started.clear()
        n = len(events)
        at(650000, "sid-r")
        ok("...and it is still crossed only once", len(events) == n)
        # …and the lines are remembered in TOKENS, not as a count of them: a session measured at 150k while its
        # window is still ASSUMED 200k is past all three of that window's lines, and reading that back as three
        # of the 1M window's lines after the correction would put the ceiling back to being the only one left.
        started.clear()
        os.environ["CC_CTX_WINDOW"] = str(DEFAULT_WINDOW)
        r = at(150000, "sid-w")
        ok("150k against an assumed 200k window is past every line that window has",
           r["window"] == DEFAULT_WINDOW and r["handed_off"] and len(started) == 1)
        started.clear()                                # the overlap dies again, and the window is corrected
        os.environ["CC_CTX_WINDOW"] = str(BIG_WINDOW)
        r = at(455000, "sid-w")
        ok("a window CORRECTION re-keys the lines: 45% of the REAL window still retries",
           r["window"] == BIG_WINDOW and events[-1][5] is True and len(started) == 1)
        os.environ.pop("CC_CTX_WINDOW")

        # ---- a cc-loop ITERATION is headless even though its window is live: the LOOP holds the track's tmux
        # window while the session inside it is `claude -p`. Reading that window as live is what opened an
        # overlap on spend-tracker and left an INTERACTIVE successor sitting in the track window long after the
        # loop had finished, with the Home tally showing the track RUNNING over a stale board (2026-09-01).
        started.clear()
        os.environ["CC_ROLE"] = "worker"
        jrn = os.path.join(STATE, "repo1", "track1", "progress.md")
        n, ne = len(started), len(events)
        r = at(420000, "sid-14")
        ok("a worker takes no phase, live window or not — no successor, and not even a fork to be told no",
           len(started) == n and len(events) == ne and not r["handed_off"])
        ok("it is told to journal and END ITS TURN: the loop's next iteration is the handoff",
           "END YOUR TURN" in r["say"] and "IS the handoff" in r["say"] and "SUCCESSOR" not in r["say"])
        ok("...and the journal is MARKED, by this file, in a line cc-loop greps — not in prose a model chose",
           open(jrn).read().startswith(MARK) and open(jrn).read().count("\n") == 1)
        r = at(320000, "sid-15")
        ok("the 30% nudge is not an ending: it says carry on, and nothing is marked",
           "carry on" in r["say"] and open(jrn).read().count("\n") == 1)
        r = at(420000, "sid-15b")
        ls = open(jrn).read().splitlines()
        ok("a second stop at the same percentage and level writes a DIFFERENT line: cc-loop's journal diff drops "
           "a byte-identical one, and the iteration would be read as a refusal again",
           len(ls) == 2 and ls[0] != ls[1] and all(x.startswith(MARK) for x in ls))
        os.environ.pop("CC_ROLE", None)
        r = at(420000, "sid-16")
        ok("a planning session in the same window still gets its successor",
           len(started) == n + 1 and r["handed_off"] and "SUCCESSOR" in r["say"])
        live[0] = False
        r = at(420000, "sid-17")
        ok("a NON-worker that cannot be restarted still hears PENDING, not END YOUR TURN (review of #164): a "
           "planning or windowless session with work in flight has no loop coming to continue it",
           "PENDING" in (r.get("say") or "") and "END YOUR TURN" not in (r.get("say") or ""))
        live[0] = True

        on[0] = False
        os.remove(os.path.join(wt, "dirty.txt"))
        have_window, event, overlap_open = hw0, ev0, oo0

        RECORDS, HANDOFF, STATE = keep, keeph, keeps

    if env0 is not None:
        os.environ["CC_CTX_WINDOW"] = env0
    if hnd0 is not None:
        os.environ["CC_HANDOFF"] = hnd0        # put the caller's own handoff back: it gates them, and it is not ours
    if role0 is not None:
        os.environ["CC_ROLE"] = role0
    if box0 is None:
        os.environ.pop("CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL", None)
    else:
        os.environ["CC_CTX_BOX_MODEL"] = box0
    # ---- --true: the pane's own number, and what happens when the pane will not give it ----------
    import contextlib, io
    PANE = """> /context
  ⎿  claude-opus-5 · 137k/1M tokens (14%)
     System prompt: 3.1k tokens
"""
    real_ask = pane_ask
    try:
        globals()["pane_ask"] = lambda target, cmd="/context": PANE
        tm = pane_measure("demo/t1")
        ok("--true reads the session's OWN number off an idle pane, window and all",
           bool(tm) and tm["used"] == 137000 and tm["window"] == 1_000_000
           and tm["pct"] == 14.0 and tm["window_src"] == "pane")
        globals()["pane_ask"] = lambda target, cmd="/context": "37k/200k tokens (18%)\n" + PANE
        tm = pane_measure("demo/t1")
        ok("…the LAST /context on the pane wins: an older one is still scrolled above the one just typed",
           bool(tm) and tm["pct"] == 14.0 and tm["window"] == 1_000_000)
        globals()["pane_ask"] = lambda target, cmd="/context": None
        ok("a pane that is busy, holding a dialog or gone gives no number at all — cc-msg --ask refused it, "
           "and a number invented here would be exactly the estimate this flag exists to replace",
           pane_measure("demo/t1") is None)

        # the fallback and the preference, through the CLI, against one transcript fixture
        px = tx([asst("claude-opus-5", 100, 0, 359_900)])
        out = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
            rc = main(["--true", "--transcript", px, "demo/t1"])
        ok("--true against a pane that refused falls back to the transcript estimate, and says the window came "
           "from the transcript's evidence rather than from the pane",
           rc == 0 and "window from" in out.getvalue() and "from pane" not in out.getvalue()
           and "36%" in out.getvalue())
        globals()["pane_ask"] = lambda target, cmd="/context": PANE
        out = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
            rc = main(["--true", "--transcript", px, "demo/t1"])
        ok("…and when the pane DOES answer, its number is the one reported — 14%, not the transcript's 36%",
           rc == 0 and "14%" in out.getvalue() and "window from pane" in out.getvalue())
        out = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
            over_true = main(["--true", "--over", "30", "-q", "--transcript", px, "demo/t1"])
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()), contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
            over_est = main(["--over", "30", "-q", "--transcript", px])
        ok("…and THE THRESHOLDS ARE DECIDED ON IT: the same transcript is over the 30% line on the estimate and "
           "under it on the pane's own reading, so --over answers differently — which is the whole point of "
           "asking the session instead of guessing at it",
           over_true == 1 and over_est == 0)
        err = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
            rc = main(["--true"])
        ok("--true with no target is an error: it types at a keyboard, and the one keyboard it must never type "
           "at is its own caller's", rc == 2 and "needs a target" in err.getvalue())
        # …and the FALLBACK belongs to the target too. No --transcript here: this is the documented bare form,
        # and resolve() with nothing to go on returns the newest transcript for whatever cwd this process is in.
        globals()["pane_ask"] = lambda target, cmd="/context": None
        real_res, seen = resolve, []
        try:
            globals()["resolve"] = lambda repo=None, track=None, sid=None, cwd=None: \
                (seen.append((repo, track, sid)) or (None, None))
            err = io.StringIO()
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
                rc = main(["--true", "demo/t1"])
            ok("a bare `--true <repo>/<track>` whose pane refuses estimates from THAT TRACK'S transcript — the "
               "one word is split back into the pair, never left as None for resolve() to answer with the "
               "CALLER'S cwd, which is a different session's percentage, level and --over answer",
               seen == [("demo", "t1", None)] and rc == 2 and "no transcript for demo/t1" in err.getvalue())
            del seen[:]
            err = io.StringIO()
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
                rc = main(["--true", "box"])
            ok("…and a target that is no <repo>/<track> at all is REFUSED rather than estimated: `box` names no "
               "board row, so there is nothing of its own to fall back to",
               seen == [] and rc == 2 and "not a <repo>/<track>" in err.getvalue())
        finally:
            globals()["resolve"] = real_res
        os.remove(px)
    finally:
        globals()["pane_ask"] = real_ask

    print(f"cc-context selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except BrokenPipeError:
        sys.exit(0)
